Subject: [PATCH] blink: derive deviceMemory from the device profile

navigator.deviceMemory is not the profile's memory figure — it is that figure
rounded to the nearer power of two and clamped to [2, 32] GiB on desktop, so the
only values a real Chrome can report are 2, 4, 8, 16 and 32.

That makes the bucketing observable, and it decides where the patch goes.
Writing a chosen value straight into approximated_device_memory_gb_ would let an
out-of-bucket number ship — a value no Chrome produces, and a worse tell than
the host's real one. Supplying the *input* and letting Chromium's own arithmetic
round it cannot fail that way.

The same reasoning puts the patch in Initialize() rather than in the Blink
accessor. ApproximatedDeviceMemory is a per-process static: the browser computes
it for the Device-Memory request header and the renderer computes it
independently for navigator.deviceMemory. Patching the input keeps those two in
agreement in whichever process runs; a renderer-only override would make the
header a server receives contradict the value JavaScript reports, which is
trivially checked server-side and invisible from inside the renderer.

Falls through to the host's real memory when no profile carries the field, per
the loader's contract: an absent field stays inherited rather than defaulted.

Ledger: navigator.device-memory, http.device-memory-header,
coh.device-memory-header-vs-js.

diff --git a/third_party/blink/common/device_memory/approximated_device_memory.cc b/third_party/blink/common/device_memory/approximated_device_memory.cc
index 7fd53eb..284ab1d 100644
--- a/third_party/blink/common/device_memory/approximated_device_memory.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/common/device_memory/approximated_device_memory.cc
@@ -4,12 +4,15 @@
 
 #include "third_party/blink/public/common/device_memory/approximated_device_memory.h"
 
+#include "base/apostate/profile.h"
 #include "base/byte_size.h"
 #include "base/check_op.h"
 #include "base/feature_list.h"
 #include "base/system/sys_info.h"
 #include "third_party/blink/public/common/features.h"
 
+#include <optional>
+
 namespace blink {
 
 // static
@@ -21,7 +24,31 @@ void ApproximatedDeviceMemory::Initialize() {
   if (approximated_device_memory_gb_ > 0.0)
     return;
   DCHECK_EQ(0, physical_memory_mb_);
-  physical_memory_mb_ = ::base::SysInfo::AmountOfTotalPhysicalMemory().InMiB();
+
+  // Apostate: feed the profile's real installed memory into the existing
+  // computation rather than overriding the value it produces.
+  //
+  // The bucketing below is observable: legal desktop results are exactly
+  // {2, 4, 8, 16, 32} GiB, because the figure is rounded to the nearer power of
+  // two and clamped. Writing a chosen value straight into
+  // approximated_device_memory_gb_ would let an out-of-bucket number ship,
+  // which no real Chrome can produce. Supplying the input and letting Chromium
+  // round it cannot.
+  //
+  // This runs in whichever process asks. That matters: the browser computes
+  // this for the Device-Memory request header and the renderer computes it
+  // independently for navigator.deviceMemory, so patching the input here keeps
+  // the two in agreement, whereas a renderer-only override would make the
+  // header contradict JavaScript.
+  const ::base::apostate::Profile* profile = ::base::apostate::Profile::Get();
+  std::optional<uint64_t> claimed =
+      profile ? profile->memory_total_bytes() : std::nullopt;
+  if (claimed.has_value()) {
+    physical_memory_mb_ = static_cast<int64_t>(*claimed / 1024 / 1024);
+  } else {
+    physical_memory_mb_ = ::base::SysInfo::AmountOfTotalPhysicalMemory().InMiB();
+  }
+
   CalculateAndSetApproximatedDeviceMemory();
 }
 
